{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdYvN6po6VSioq7smrZh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Website operators who want to limit the use of their content for AI training currently have to manage that preference one crawler at a time. On October 30, OpenAI described that process: operators identify individual crawlers and keep separate rules for each provider. The company announced that it will honor a common robots.txt signal intended to replace much of that repetitive work.\n\nThe signal lets an operator state a single preference about foundational generative AI training that applies to every participating provider. It comes from the IETF's AI Preferences working group, and other participants in that group joined OpenAI in announcing it. OpenAI calls the arrangement voluntary and encourages other AI providers to honor the signal, so the time it saves an operator will depend on how many providers sign on.\n\nNone of this changes what operators should do today. OpenAI says it aims to implement the signal in the coming months and will publish implementation details at that point. Its existing robots.txt crawler controls will continue to apply after the new signal arrives. Operators' current OpenAI-specific rules therefore remain the mechanism that counts, both now and later. The open questions concern the implementation details OpenAI has promised and which providers beyond the announcing group will adopt the signal. Answers to those questions will show how much of the per-crawler work a single line in robots.txt can replace."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":727,"cache_read_input_tokens":5283,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":727},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics